1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:03,140
The ones closely kept secrets of body preservation.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,000
Now, Taxidermy on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:24,140
Each year in Alaska, the Department of Fish and Game grants an average of 700 permits to hunt Kodiak bears

4
00:00:24,141 --> 00:00:26,340
in an effort to control the population.

5
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:28,780
He's big. He's over 9 foot.

6
00:00:28,781 --> 00:00:37,000
The fee is $500, but Joe Coolis of Bedford, Ohio has spent an additional $5,000 in expenses to take his shot.

7
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:39,740
Take him now, Joe.

8
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,600
He's down. He's hit hard. He's on his back in a hole.

9
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,840
Coolis isn't here to bring food back for the family table.

10
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:52,100
He's only after the skin and pelt.

11
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:56,340
With at least two additional bears circling the kill,

12
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,900
Kulis must quickly skin and flesh the Kodiak's hide.

13
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:04,840
The massive bundle of fur now begins a remarkable journey

14
00:01:04,841 --> 00:01:06,980
that will take it first to the tanner,

15
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,040
where the skin is salted, pickled,

16
00:01:09,460 --> 00:01:11,820
and turned into a soft, nearly permanent leather.

17
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,360
Then it's shipped to Kulis' own taxidermy shop and studio.

18
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,560
Here it's fitted over a foam form and glass eyes.

19
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:27,780
The finished mount is an uncanny match for the bare coolest bagged in the frozen north.

20
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:32,160
And now you have a life-size photograph, so to speak.

21
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,640
You can look and say, oh, I remember when that happened and this happened.

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,860
And then a lot of times it's definitely dangerous.

23
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:41,040
So I think it's the accomplishment of what you have to go through

24
00:01:41,041 --> 00:01:46,920
and then achieving something, then reproducing it so it looks exactly like it did when alive.

25
00:01:46,921 --> 00:01:50,820
You put all those things together and now you have something and think, wow, that was really great.

26
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:56,880
Very few people are neutral when confronted with a once living head hanging from a wall,

27
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,580
or a giant predator frozen at the moment of attack.

28
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:07,640
Many find the ancient practice of mounting animals for display disturbing and downright macabre.

29
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:12,740
Enthusiasts of taxidermy, however, feel a thrill

30
00:02:12,741 --> 00:02:17,600
and are in awe of what could be described as still lives of nature, perfectly preserved.

31
00:02:17,601 --> 00:02:25,040
When you get into taxidermy, it encompasses so many other kinds of work.

32
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:31,880
Anatomy, biology, chemistry, woodworking, natural design.

33
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:40,360
So when you put that all together under the word taxidermy, you can't help but not encompass the word art with it.

34
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,620
The practice of taxidermy is undeniably popular.

35
00:02:44,621 --> 00:02:48,180
Experts estimate the number of practicing taxidermists

36
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:51,520
to be as high as 35,000 in the United States alone.

37
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,760
I tell them what I do and they take a step back away from me

38
00:02:55,761 --> 00:02:58,600
and some people scream and run away

39
00:02:58,601 --> 00:03:02,820
and mostly women are very upset by the fact,

40
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,480
but I see it as a very creative profession

41
00:03:06,481 --> 00:03:11,220
and I have a great deal of respect and love for the animals that I work on.

42
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:12,840
We aren't barbarians.

43
00:03:12,841 --> 00:03:16,240
You know, our industry, our artistry is not antiquated.

44
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:20,240
There was a, you know, recent kids' film, that 102 Dalmatians,

45
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:23,780
where, you know, there was a taxidermist that was used.

46
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:25,040
He was the evil villain.

47
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:27,420
And that's certainly not what we are at all.

48
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,820
We like to think of ourselves as professionals and artists,

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,060
and we're doing a public service.

50
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:38,260
The origins of taxidermy are tied to some of our first basic needs,

51
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:40,040
clothing and shelter.

52
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:42,280
I have to go back to Genesis.

53
00:03:42,281 --> 00:03:51,620
When Adam and Eve were banned from the Garden of Eden, the Lord God himself took the very first animal skins and fashioned clothing for Adam and Eve to wear.

54
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:58,120
The need for animal skins for clothing and shelter soon led directly to the first crude forms of taxidermy.

55
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:04,700
Primitive man did taxidermy by learning that he needed to come up with a decoy.

56
00:04:04,701 --> 00:04:09,620
An animal hide draped over a rock, bush, or even the hunter himself

57
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:12,720
allowed us to get in close with spears and knives.

58
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:16,920
Remember, there wasn't a loud noise of gunshot.

59
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,780
Everything was all close to encounter.

60
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:23,100
So they had to be very, very close to make these little flint tools do their work.

61
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:28,240
For taxidermy to evolve beyond the ability to fool animals,

62
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:31,600
a permanency would need to be added to the furs and hides.

63
00:04:31,601 --> 00:04:39,060
This process of chemically transforming skin into leather would come to be known as tanning.

64
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:45,880
I think one of the most amazing testaments that we have in our time is the Iceman found in the Swiss Alps.

65
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,120
5,000-year-old corpse who had leather shoes, leather bow quiver.

66
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:58,400
Preserved hides now gave us a better chance against sometimes overwhelming elements.

67
00:04:58,401 --> 00:05:06,260
but we can go back as far as the Egyptians they had a process of tanning animal skin into leather

68
00:05:06,261 --> 00:05:13,220
not only were the Egyptians adept at tanning they developed some of the first practices of

69
00:05:13,221 --> 00:05:22,520
body preservation and their work was not confined to humans mummified cats dogs apes and oxen have

70
00:05:22,521 --> 00:05:27,212
found in royal tombs alongside preserved kings and slaves.

71
00:05:27,213 --> 00:05:29,800
Pets were primarily mummified to

72
00:05:29,801 --> 00:05:33,680
serve members of the royal clan in the afterlife.

73
00:05:33,681 --> 00:05:37,640
Body preservation was discovered quite by accident

74
00:05:37,641 --> 00:05:42,680
when it was noticed that animals and humans who died in arid conditions didn't quickly decompose.

75
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:49,240
Ridding a body or hide of fluids was the first step toward preserving a skin through time.

76
00:05:49,241 --> 00:05:56,600
Later, across the Mediterranean, Greeks and Romans were using preservation techniques to

77
00:05:56,601 --> 00:06:00,055
make different styles of sandals, boots and shoes.

78
00:06:00,056 --> 00:06:03,440
They used barks, fruit peels and leaves as tannin

79
00:06:03,441 --> 00:06:10,128
sources. These agents render the leather immune to decay and shrinkage.

80
00:06:10,129 --> 00:06:12,200
A tour around a modern

81
00:06:12,201 --> 00:06:17,280
tannery reveals that the techniques for processing skin into leather have been refined but have

82
00:06:17,281 --> 00:06:19,040
remained essentially unchanged.

83
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:22,840
A Greek, Roman, or Egyptian tanner would quickly become very comfortable

84
00:06:22,841 --> 00:06:25,040
with the equipment and tanning agents found here.

85
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:30,800
Before these hides can be mounted by a taxidermist,

86
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:32,500
they must be processed into leather.

87
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:37,740
This is the stock room where we store all the skins that are delivered to the tannery.

88
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:40,320
Most of this stuff here is deer and elk.

89
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:43,760
Right here is a Cape buffalo half mount.

90
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:46,940
This is the head part, the feet, and the back of the hide.

91
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:53,680
And over here, we have a giraffe leg.

92
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,820
It came in pieces. We have four legs.

93
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:00,560
We had the cape, and we had the hide over here.

94
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:07,000
Before hides are tanned, excess layers of skin are cut away in a process called thinning.

95
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:13,380
On large games, such as elephant, rhino, or hippo, skin can be several inches thick.

96
00:07:13,381 --> 00:07:20,380
It must be brought down to less than a quarter inch, so that the tanning solutions can be evenly absorbed.

97
00:07:20,381 --> 00:07:24,380
This hippo hide is being cut down with an ancient looking skiving knife.

98
00:07:24,381 --> 00:07:27,380
For large animals this process can take weeks.

99
00:07:27,381 --> 00:07:32,380
The shavers have to break the resistance of the skin, but they can't make it too thin or too thick.

100
00:07:32,381 --> 00:07:36,380
If too thick they won't get no stretch out of it, too thin the hair will fall out.

101
00:07:36,381 --> 00:07:41,380
After being shaved, skins are tossed into large vats filled with alum and salt.

102
00:07:41,381 --> 00:07:45,820
salt. Alum draws out the living enzymes in the skin, turning it into leather.

103
00:07:45,821 --> 00:07:49,020
The skin is going that wet drum with the picolone agent.

104
00:07:49,021 --> 00:07:50,620
Once they come out of there,

105
00:07:50,621 --> 00:07:57,100
they're actually leather. From tanning to taxidermy, preserved furs could now be

106
00:07:57,101 --> 00:08:03,147
pulled over forms of wood and straw to create semi-permanent figures for display.

107
00:08:03,148 --> 00:08:03,848
These

108
00:08:03,849 --> 00:08:08,533
early under frames were often made by upholsterers and furniture builders.

109
00:08:08,534 --> 00:08:09,820
In these very early

110
00:08:09,821 --> 00:08:14,116
frames. The actual skulls of the animals being mounted were used.

111
00:08:14,117 --> 00:08:15,900
The skull of the animal was

112
00:08:15,901 --> 00:08:21,816
boiled and cleaned and scraped full of flesh and tissue. A wooden armature was set.

113
00:08:21,817 --> 00:08:22,600
A baseboard

114
00:08:22,601 --> 00:08:24,877
was shaped to accommodate the skull.

115
00:08:24,878 --> 00:08:28,420
Add some support for the building materials, which would

116
00:08:28,421 --> 00:08:34,730
be Excelsior, cotton, straw, wrapped with twine to fill the voids.

117
00:08:34,731 --> 00:08:37,120
That was known as the old

118
00:08:37,121 --> 00:08:43,949
English style of taxidermy. Early bird under mounts were even simpler.

119
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:45,900
Here's just a couple

120
00:08:45,901 --> 00:08:50,631
examples of a typical structure that's inside of bird mounts.

121
00:08:50,632 --> 00:08:52,880
In most older museums we have

122
00:08:52,881 --> 00:08:57,500
these traditional mannequins that are put together with wire. Some of the original

123
00:08:57,501 --> 00:09:00,326
parts stay inside, like the legs for example.

124
00:09:00,327 --> 00:09:03,340
These are the real legs, wires run through them.

125
00:09:03,341 --> 00:09:08,340
Small, delicate body parts like bird legs and feet can't be tanned.

126
00:09:08,341 --> 00:09:14,340
They can, however, be drilled hollow, flushed, and coated inside and out with a preservative.

127
00:09:14,341 --> 00:09:23,340
Beginning in the early 1800s, the best way to deter anything from beetles to moths to rats was deadly arsenic.

128
00:09:23,341 --> 00:09:30,340
Arsenic was the most major advancement the industry of taxidermy, I think, ever had.

129
00:09:30,341 --> 00:09:37,140
had because now we had a way to stop infestation of vermin in our finished work.

130
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:43,880
Credited to a 19th century French taxidermist, Louis Dufresne, the preservative had a distinct

131
00:09:43,881 --> 00:09:44,581
drawback.

132
00:09:44,582 --> 00:09:49,640
It was as poisonous to the taxidermist as it was to the pests he was trying to keep away

133
00:09:49,641 --> 00:09:50,680
from his work.

134
00:09:50,681 --> 00:09:56,920
Some of us got poisoned by arsenic, some of us died by arsenic, okay, and some of us

135
00:09:56,921 --> 00:10:00,455
just couldn't live without it. I still use arsenic myself.

136
00:10:00,456 --> 00:10:02,100
There are certain things I,

137
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:08,900
when I do a particularly rare piece or something where I know my integrity is on the line and I

138
00:10:08,901 --> 00:10:14,120
know it won't be subject to people touching it, it'll be put behind glass, I will use arsenic

139
00:10:14,121 --> 00:10:16,841
myself to this day. No question about it.

140
00:10:16,842 --> 00:10:19,960
As taxidermists began to produce more permanent

141
00:10:19,961 --> 00:10:24,660
and more anatomically correct mounts, they slowly found a place of respect in the world

142
00:10:24,661 --> 00:10:30,100
of natural history. President Thomas Jefferson had explorer Meriwether Lewis

143
00:10:30,101 --> 00:10:33,780
tutored in taxidermy before leaving on the groundbreaking Lewis and Clark

144
00:10:33,781 --> 00:10:40,200
expedition in 1804. Thomas Jefferson sent him down to Philadelphia to learn

145
00:10:40,201 --> 00:10:46,160
preservation techniques, maritime education as well, medical practices for

146
00:10:46,161 --> 00:10:50,960
medical emergencies. All this was placed on a young Meriwether Lewis. Lewis's

147
00:10:50,961 --> 00:10:52,764
taxidermy survives to this day.

148
00:10:52,765 --> 00:10:56,720
A still well-preserved woodpecker currently resides with the Harvard

149
00:10:56,721 --> 00:10:58,423
Museum of Natural History.

150
00:10:58,424 --> 00:11:02,680
But it was a very unscientific endeavor that would give taxidermy

151
00:11:02,681 --> 00:11:05,874
its next push forward. Legendary showman P.T.

152
00:11:05,875 --> 00:11:09,280
Barnum and his mammoth elephant Jumbo would pave

153
00:11:09,281 --> 00:11:13,131
the way for the great museums of the 20th century.

154
00:11:13,132 --> 00:11:17,060
The word taxidermy originates from two Greek words,

155
00:11:17,061 --> 00:11:20,620
Taxis, meaning movement, and derma, meaning skin.

156
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:23,620
Taxidermy will return on Modern Marvels.

157
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:27,720
Turn to taxidermy on Modern Marvels.

158
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:40,240
The Field Museum in Chicago represents the golden age of scientific taxidermy.

159
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:44,200
Visitors to the museum are confronted with fighting elephants,

160
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:46,920
posed in combat for well over 100 years.

161
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:54,060
The figures in the Four Seasons of the Deer, commissioned in 1900, still interact in accurately

162
00:11:54,061 --> 00:11:55,060
depicted dioramas.

163
00:11:55,061 --> 00:12:01,060
And the man-eating lions of Tsavo still look hungrily out at museum visitors.

164
00:12:01,061 --> 00:12:06,060
But there's an even bigger taxidermy operation behind the scenes.

165
00:12:06,061 --> 00:12:14,060
It's the specimens in these drawers that will aid scientists in uncovering and unlocking

166
00:12:14,061 --> 00:12:17,980
the huge number of mysteries that still surround us regarding this planet.

167
00:12:17,981 --> 00:12:23,500
And then ultimately, whatever is learned by studying those mysteries will be displayed downstairs.

168
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:27,620
Because one of the main missions of this museum is public education

169
00:12:27,621 --> 00:12:31,780
and taking what we learn upstairs and telling the public about it accurately.

170
00:12:32,460 --> 00:12:35,800
The Field Museum was the birthplace of modern taxidermy,

171
00:12:36,180 --> 00:12:38,360
under the direction of innovator Carl Akeley.

172
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:41,980
He was essentially the father of modern taxidermy.

173
00:12:41,981 --> 00:12:46,021
taxidermy and even calling him that doesn't give him the credit that's due.

174
00:12:46,022 --> 00:12:47,260
He was the first person

175
00:12:47,261 --> 00:12:52,542
to actually mount animals such that they were in their natural habitat.

176
00:12:52,543 --> 00:12:54,700
When you saw an Akeley mount,

177
00:12:54,701 --> 00:13:00,260
you saw what the animal looked like in real life and you saw that not only in the color of the skin,

178
00:13:00,840 --> 00:13:02,614
you saw it in the shape of a muscle.

179
00:13:02,615 --> 00:13:05,620
These are art pieces just like people go to the Louvre to see

180
00:13:05,621 --> 00:13:06,322
the Mona Lisa.

181
00:13:06,323 --> 00:13:09,980
Many people come here to see Carl Akeley's work because he was an artist.

182
00:13:09,981 --> 00:13:16,680
Hickley is credited with perfecting the sculptured hollow mannequin technique of mounting life-sized animals.

183
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:22,720
First, the skeleton of the skinned animal would be reassembled.

184
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:28,440
Next, the musculature would be sculpted in great detail from measurements and notes taken in the field.

185
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:31,040
Then the figure would be molded in plaster.

186
00:13:32,140 --> 00:13:36,720
When dried and pulled apart, the interior of the mold would be coated with burlap and glue.

187
00:13:36,721 --> 00:13:43,960
The lightweight burlap mount that emerged from the mold would be a nearly perfect duplicate of the animal's original body.

188
00:13:46,020 --> 00:13:53,900
These revolutionary ideas were already in the back of the mind of Carl Akeley at the age of 19, when an amazing opportunity befell him.

189
00:13:54,640 --> 00:14:03,520
His employer at the time, taxidermist Henry Ward, was called in to salvage the remains of a circus elephant hit by a train on September 15, 1885.

190
00:14:03,521 --> 00:14:08,520
Young Carl was sent to St. Thomas, Ontario to see what, if anything, could be done.

191
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:13,520
Jumbo was the star of P.T. Barnum's greatest show on earth.

192
00:14:13,521 --> 00:14:20,520
At a height of 11 and a half feet and a weight of over six tons, he represented a truly formidable taxidermy project.

193
00:14:20,521 --> 00:14:25,520
Again, that period of time, no such thing as Stuttle Express, no tractor trailers.

194
00:14:25,521 --> 00:14:30,520
By the time they got to the point where the elephant died, it was half deteriorated.

195
00:14:30,521 --> 00:14:33,850
deteriorated. It was almost decomposed.

196
00:14:33,851 --> 00:14:38,460
Phineas Barnum had already made his name in taxidermy.

197
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:43,960
His American Museum, a permanent freak show, an emporium of oddities, which entertained the

198
00:14:43,961 --> 00:14:50,600
general public between 1841 and 1865, demonstrated that the public had a paying interest in taxidermy.

199
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:53,370
His interest was never scientific.

200
00:14:53,371 --> 00:14:56,260
He would exhibit real animals opposite such imagined

201
00:14:56,261 --> 00:14:58,163
oddities is the Fiji mermaid.

202
00:14:58,164 --> 00:15:02,100
This specimen is actually a monkey body sewn to a fish tail.

203
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:08,420
Pornham believed crowds would also flock to the mounted body of Jumbo.

204
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:11,300
The methods used were the best in the day.

205
00:15:12,980 --> 00:15:21,140
They made a framework of wood, tons and tons of iron nails, wooden lats to take up the space of

206
00:15:21,141 --> 00:15:27,620
ribs and bone, wire cloth, plaster of Paris, paper mache, more Excelsior, and just constantly

207
00:15:27,621 --> 00:15:32,841
doing this until the skin finally fit. Barnum's hunch was correct.

208
00:15:32,842 --> 00:15:34,740
Displayed beside his own

209
00:15:34,741 --> 00:15:39,007
reassembled skeleton, Jumbo did become a bigger draw after death.

210
00:15:39,008 --> 00:15:40,780
Carl Akeley was on his way.

211
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:47,180
Akeley's first museum work was for the Milwaukee Public Museum, and he joined Chicago's Field

212
00:15:47,181 --> 00:15:48,307
Museum in 1906.

213
00:15:48,308 --> 00:15:54,020
Here and later at the American Museum of Natural History in New York, Akeley

214
00:15:54,021 --> 00:15:58,440
not only perfected the look of the animal, he raised the art of the diorama to a new

215
00:15:58,441 --> 00:15:59,141
level.

216
00:15:59,142 --> 00:16:03,740
These scenes, based on the paintings of artists sent on location, are accurate

217
00:16:03,741 --> 00:16:06,008
down to the tiniest plants.

218
00:16:06,009 --> 00:16:10,880
He and his wife Delia worked incredible hours casting each

219
00:16:10,881 --> 00:16:13,483
and every one of the individual leaves.

220
00:16:13,484 --> 00:16:16,620
So he took great pains to recreate not only the

221
00:16:16,621 --> 00:16:20,780
animal as accurately as possible, but the habitat that that animal came from.

222
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:25,120
Akeley's attention to detail would cost him over his lifetime.

223
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:29,200
He insisted on studying his subjects and their natural environments.

224
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:32,300
Carl Akeley was attacked twice in Africa.

225
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:36,940
Once by a leopard that he thought was a warthog, turned out to be a leopard.

226
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:39,560
And the leopard hurt him pretty badly.

227
00:16:39,561 --> 00:16:41,640
The second time was when he went hunting elephants.

228
00:16:41,641 --> 00:16:46,720
He just got too close to a wounded animal and the elephant actually pinned him to the ground.

229
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:54,880
And fortunately for Carl Akeley, the one tusk of the animal hit a rock or a root several feet into the dirt

230
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:58,680
that stopped that massive forehead from crushing his chest.

231
00:16:59,740 --> 00:17:06,800
But if you saw pictures of him afterwards, you wouldn't know if he went through a plane crash or war or why.

232
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:10,140
But he really, really, really came close to losing his life with the elephant.

233
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:16,060
Hickley died in Africa in 1926, possibly a victim of his passion for his craft.

234
00:17:17,420 --> 00:17:27,600
Described as a workaholic, it's believed the cause of his death was exhaustion compounded by a lifetime working with a taxidermist's deadly preservative, arsenic.

235
00:17:29,720 --> 00:17:36,500
Still, he did pass on his dream of bringing fantastic scenes of the four corners of the world to the American public.

236
00:17:36,501 --> 00:17:45,500
Hickley's work inspired many taxidermists, including the 26th president, Theodore Roosevelt.

237
00:17:45,501 --> 00:17:51,500
As a young boy with many health problems, Roosevelt found release in taxidermy.

238
00:17:51,501 --> 00:18:00,500
These birds, mounted by young Roosevelt in Egypt in 1872, reside at his birthplace in New York.

239
00:18:00,501 --> 00:18:07,920
As president, Roosevelt brought his love of taxidermy to the White House.

240
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:13,420
When he was with his cabinet, he was suddenly stopped the meeting and listened.

241
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:18,220
He heard a particular wobbler or he heard a particular songbird.

242
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:24,040
And to be able to identify what that was by what he heard stopped the meeting in progress.

243
00:18:24,380 --> 00:18:29,240
He always wanted to be where natural history was, where adventure was.

244
00:18:29,241 --> 00:18:32,320
and fortunately for him, he had the mean to do it all.

245
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,580
He took a really long, extensive trip to Canada

246
00:18:35,581 --> 00:18:39,540
and literally put a bearskin rug in front of every office in the White House,

247
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:42,860
as well as mounting one for his own trophy room.

248
00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:47,080
Another inspired young man, Louis Paul Jonas,

249
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:50,160
would help make taxidermy available to the hobbyist.

250
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:53,340
Jonas worked with Carl Akeley in New York.

251
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:56,720
Here, he mounts an elephant the Akeley Way,

252
00:18:57,380 --> 00:19:00,760
first sculpting an underbody in clay over a wood and iron form,

253
00:19:02,020 --> 00:19:03,260
next fitting the skin,

254
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:07,660
then creating a plaster cast of the fitted skin,

255
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:10,500
which would be used to create a papier-mâché undermount,

256
00:19:12,440 --> 00:19:15,440
and then refitting the skin for final display.

257
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:24,220
In 1908, Louis Paul Jonas struck out with brothers John, Coleman, and Guy

258
00:19:24,221 --> 00:19:29,040
to open their own taxidermy shop in the strategically located city of Denver, Colorado.

259
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:32,460
All the trains came into Denver.

260
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:37,980
Hunters would come through first, and, you know, they'd have a couple hours to roam around,

261
00:19:38,100 --> 00:19:40,520
and Granddad would hand out the price list.

262
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:42,480
And, of course, a deer head was very expensive.

263
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:43,300
That was $10.

264
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:47,340
Now they're $520 plus.

265
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:52,140
Jack Jonas, a successful animal appraiser,

266
00:19:52,141 --> 00:19:56,540
was present as his grandfather and great-uncles grew their successful shop

267
00:19:56,541 --> 00:19:59,560
into one of the largest taxidermy supply companies in the world.

268
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:04,560
One time, we had 110 employees.

269
00:20:05,340 --> 00:20:07,100
That's big for a taxidermy company.

270
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:11,880
And we did, oh, close to $4.5 million,

271
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:16,880
but that covered the taxidermy, the tanning department,

272
00:20:16,881 --> 00:20:22,560
the supplies which were selling all the taxidermists there,

273
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:27,400
mannequins and pliers and hammers and nails and cords and anything they needed.

274
00:20:28,080 --> 00:20:32,180
Today, Jonas Brothers continues to provide everything from full taxidermy

275
00:20:32,181 --> 00:20:35,140
to parts like teeth and eyes and tools.

276
00:20:35,320 --> 00:20:38,560
Well, literally, we sell to taxidermists around the world,

277
00:20:38,740 --> 00:20:43,540
from all walks of life, from hobbyists to full-time working professionals.

278
00:20:43,541 --> 00:20:48,400
Jonas Brothers may have scaled down from a work staff of 110,

279
00:20:48,401 --> 00:20:51,820
but it's still a testament to the popularity of taxidermy.

280
00:20:51,821 --> 00:20:55,660
Parts and tools are still a big part of the business,

281
00:20:55,661 --> 00:21:00,460
but cloth, plaster and paper molds have given way to dense polyurethane foam.

282
00:21:00,461 --> 00:21:04,300
For creating the form of a mannequin, initially we start off with a clay sculpture

283
00:21:04,301 --> 00:21:11,320
that is either started from either a carcass cast or sculpted right over like the actual skull or skeleton of an animal.

284
00:21:11,321 --> 00:21:17,760
From that sculpture, we then make a fiberglass mold, which becomes our master for creating forms.

285
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:23,740
The mold is resimuled, and then we shoot the urethane foam into that mold, and that creates the form.

286
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:30,780
Traditional displays are being challenged in popularity by new dramatic poses,

287
00:21:31,460 --> 00:21:35,720
some inspired by nature, and some drawn more from the taxidermist's imagination.

288
00:21:36,760 --> 00:21:40,500
There are also customers with interests that border on the bizarre.

289
00:21:41,200 --> 00:21:44,380
This bear will be posed to greet guests with a silver serving tray.

290
00:21:44,920 --> 00:21:46,820
This baboon will also work as a greeter.

291
00:21:47,060 --> 00:21:49,280
This is just going to be for a conversation piece.

292
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:51,280
This little guy will be holding an ostrich egg.

293
00:21:51,340 --> 00:21:53,440
Sometimes we do serving trays on that.

294
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:57,320
Like this is actually an artificial egg that we make.

295
00:21:58,180 --> 00:22:02,920
And so his arms will be spread open like this, and then he'll...

296
00:22:02,921 --> 00:22:04,240
There you go, just like that.

297
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:06,460
Voila!

298
00:22:06,461 --> 00:22:12,440
Perhaps one of the oddest requests the Jonas Brothers ever received

299
00:22:12,441 --> 00:22:15,560
was from 40s and 50s cowboy movie star Roy Rogers.

300
00:22:16,420 --> 00:22:19,140
Rogers was an avid hunter who had big game mounts

301
00:22:19,141 --> 00:22:21,220
throughout his Victorville, California ranch.

302
00:22:22,320 --> 00:22:24,440
Roy decided he wanted his movie horse, Trigger,

303
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:26,160
and his movie dog, Bullet,

304
00:22:26,580 --> 00:22:28,980
preserved with taxidermy at the times of their deaths.

305
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:31,960
I got to know Roy pretty well

306
00:22:31,961 --> 00:22:35,960
because I spent about five weeks up into the Arctic Circle.

307
00:22:35,961 --> 00:22:39,500
We had 21 days where there was no hunting at all.

308
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:42,840
And so I got to know Roy quite well.

309
00:22:43,100 --> 00:22:46,120
He called me one day and asked me whether we would mount Trigger,

310
00:22:46,280 --> 00:22:48,220
and Roy was excited as hell.

311
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:52,820
The Jonas Company ended up passing on the job,

312
00:22:53,160 --> 00:22:56,620
but Trigger and Bullitt were mounted by Bischoff's Taxidermy of California

313
00:22:56,621 --> 00:23:01,560
and are currently on display at the Roy Rogers Day-11s Museum in Branson, Missouri.

314
00:23:01,561 --> 00:23:06,980
Good taxidermy creates a moment frozen in time

315
00:23:06,981 --> 00:23:10,240
And that's exactly the next leap the art form takes

316
00:23:10,241 --> 00:23:14,620
As freeze-drying becomes the next tool in the arsenal of the taxidermist

317
00:23:14,621 --> 00:23:21,620
The hide of Jumbo the Elephant weighed an estimated 1,500 pounds

318
00:23:21,621 --> 00:23:25,500
Taxidermy will return on Modern Marvels

319
00:23:25,501 --> 00:23:29,680
At Extreme Taxidermy on Modern Marvels

320
00:23:31,560 --> 00:23:38,560
In Bedford, Ohio, the coolest taxidermy company is crammed full of exotic animals.

321
00:23:38,561 --> 00:23:46,560
Rhino, bear, fox, leopard and boar crowd the walkways and counters.

322
00:23:46,561 --> 00:23:51,560
Even the attic is filled with moose, deer, rhino and hippo.

323
00:23:51,561 --> 00:23:54,560
Most have been preserved using traditional techniques,

324
00:23:54,561 --> 00:24:01,560
but some have attained their physical immortality with a relatively new process called sublimation or freeze drying.

325
00:24:01,561 --> 00:24:07,960
Here we have a freeze-dried snapping turtle, and actually freeze-drying is the best way to do it because there's no skinning involved.

326
00:24:08,300 --> 00:24:14,880
Freeze-drying is basically just posing it, and you could make it look as mean or as docile as you want by what you do with the posing.

327
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:16,360
But this will last forever.

328
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:22,980
Of course, it is like a rock, and it is definitely the way to do turtles, reptiles, things like that.

329
00:24:22,981 --> 00:24:29,140
The idea actually goes back to the ancient Incas, who would store goods at high altitudes

330
00:24:29,141 --> 00:24:34,940
where they would freeze and then dehydrate in the low pressure.

331
00:24:34,941 --> 00:24:39,420
Human remains from ritual sacrifices have also been discovered at these altitudes.

332
00:24:39,421 --> 00:24:44,180
500 years old and in amazing condition.

333
00:24:44,181 --> 00:24:47,920
Freeze drying has been used in more recent times to preserve blood plasma and penicillin

334
00:24:47,921 --> 00:24:50,920
during World War II.

335
00:24:50,921 --> 00:24:56,880
In the 1960s, freeze drying took the Army K-ration into a new era.

336
00:24:56,881 --> 00:25:01,200
Based on work done at NASA for astronauts with their sights set on the moon, soldiers

337
00:25:01,201 --> 00:25:06,940
could now carry lightweight packets, which became meals when reintroduced to water.

338
00:25:06,941 --> 00:25:11,360
Cold water is added to the food bar.

339
00:25:11,361 --> 00:25:15,620
There they are, pieces of chicken, carrots, peas.

340
00:25:15,621 --> 00:25:18,260
The bar is reversibly compressed.

341
00:25:18,261 --> 00:25:23,560
the addition of water, its ingredients are reconstituted to their original size, shape,

342
00:25:23,860 --> 00:25:25,277
texture, and taste.

343
00:25:25,278 --> 00:25:30,720
At the Smithsonian Institute in Washington, D.C., during the same decade,

344
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:34,600
experiments in freeze-drying for taxidermy began.

345
00:25:34,601 --> 00:25:37,680
Joe Coolis flew to Washington to study the new

346
00:25:37,681 --> 00:25:42,500
process, then set up one of the first commercial freeze-drying operations at his shop.

347
00:25:42,501 --> 00:25:46,200
I wanted to do this commercially. I need to have some volume.

348
00:25:46,201 --> 00:25:51,960
So I basically put a dish on the building, bought a freezer, 12 by 12 by 30,

349
00:25:52,340 --> 00:25:54,260
so I'd have plenty of room for four tanks,

350
00:25:54,580 --> 00:25:58,980
found the lead on four pressure cooker tanks from a cannery in New York,

351
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:02,080
and basically built my freezer around these machines.

352
00:26:03,680 --> 00:26:05,480
This is how the process works.

353
00:26:06,140 --> 00:26:09,280
A posed unfrozen animal is placed into a vacuum chamber,

354
00:26:09,281 --> 00:26:12,960
where the temperature is lowered to the point at which the animal freezes solid.

355
00:26:14,300 --> 00:26:17,540
Next, pumps are engaged, removing all air from the chamber.

356
00:26:18,940 --> 00:26:22,800
Throughout the animal's cells, frozen water now escapes as a gas,

357
00:26:23,100 --> 00:26:24,780
completely skipping the liquid stage.

358
00:26:25,320 --> 00:26:29,000
And it goes from an ice crystal to a gas with nothing in between.

359
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:35,280
So if you boil water right here in Ohio, it boils at 212 degrees.

360
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:38,820
You go to Denver, it probably boils at the mid-200s.

361
00:26:38,821 --> 00:26:41,820
You go to Mount Everest, it might be a boil at 190.

362
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:45,080
The higher you go in elevation, the lower the boiling point.

363
00:26:45,480 --> 00:26:49,380
So if you go to outer space, is what we reproduce in the chamber,

364
00:26:49,820 --> 00:26:53,520
is water is boiling away at 40, 50 below zero.

365
00:26:54,320 --> 00:26:58,120
What remains are cells that are intact in composition and structure.

366
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:02,200
The animal holds its pose now completely devoid of water.

367
00:27:04,660 --> 00:27:06,440
Now I'll show you what the freeze dryer's like.

368
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:08,500
A little cold in here.

369
00:27:08,820 --> 00:27:15,820
It's full of ice of all kinds.

370
00:27:18,820 --> 00:27:23,820
And in here we've got deer that are in the velvet, which is really the only way to freeze-dry

371
00:27:23,821 --> 00:27:28,820
to preserve velvet on horns. We have a lot of fish, there's a bass.

372
00:27:28,821 --> 00:27:34,820
We drill holes in the back to speed it up so the moisture can release and freeze-dry sooner.

373
00:27:34,821 --> 00:27:36,960
sooner but it's great for these little fish

374
00:27:36,961 --> 00:27:43,020
there's probably about a 25-30 pound muskie

375
00:27:43,021 --> 00:27:49,900
and right now there's a lot of fish in the machine but as you can see with the size of it

376
00:27:49,901 --> 00:27:54,480
we can get in something pretty large whether it be a small pony like we've done

377
00:27:54,481 --> 00:27:59,740
people really think that freeze-drike is really quick but a fish this size

378
00:27:59,741 --> 00:28:04,480
this five pound bass is probably going to take about five to six weeks in the machine

379
00:28:04,481 --> 00:28:06,660
Then over here, there's another chamber.

380
00:28:06,960 --> 00:28:10,720
We're also doing processing of meat for the food bank and for hunters,

381
00:28:11,120 --> 00:28:12,780
but we do a lot of crickets.

382
00:28:13,400 --> 00:28:15,120
These crickets are frozen now.

383
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:16,760
You can see they're like a rock.

384
00:28:17,340 --> 00:28:21,020
When they'll come out of the freeze dryer, they'll just be loose like grains of sand.

385
00:28:21,840 --> 00:28:24,400
The crickets will be sold as pet food for lizards,

386
00:28:24,820 --> 00:28:27,360
just one of the side jobs a freeze dryer can pick up.

387
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:31,860
Joe has also used the vacuum chamber to restore water-damaged documents.

388
00:28:32,500 --> 00:28:34,960
We can take a soggy book.

389
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:37,720
We have one now that is from 1600s.

390
00:28:37,820 --> 00:28:39,120
It was solid wet.

391
00:28:39,620 --> 00:28:41,000
They could do absolutely nothing with it.

392
00:28:41,240 --> 00:28:43,100
We could take it, put it in a freeze dryer.

393
00:28:43,500 --> 00:28:46,500
It might get a little wrinkly, but you could open through every page.

394
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:51,480
Joe has another side business in freeze drying wedding and funeral flowers.

395
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:53,960
They, too, will last virtually forever.

396
00:28:54,920 --> 00:28:59,680
But perhaps his most unusual freeze drying endeavor comes right back around to taxidermy.

397
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:02,120
Joe freeze-drys family pets.

398
00:29:02,121 --> 00:29:05,560
People send us pets basically from all over the country

399
00:29:05,561 --> 00:29:07,920
because people are attached to pets

400
00:29:07,921 --> 00:29:09,620
because some don't have children,

401
00:29:09,621 --> 00:29:11,260
but they've had it a lot of years,

402
00:29:11,261 --> 00:29:12,800
and they've become attached to it.

403
00:29:12,801 --> 00:29:15,800
They'd rather see that pet and be able to look at it

404
00:29:15,801 --> 00:29:18,640
and say, wow, that's, it's in their mind, it's still there.

405
00:29:18,641 --> 00:29:20,300
It's better than a photograph,

406
00:29:20,301 --> 00:29:23,740
and it's odd that it's probably split 50-50.

407
00:29:23,741 --> 00:29:25,400
Some people think that that's horrible,

408
00:29:25,401 --> 00:29:28,440
and the other 50%, wow, can I have your card?

409
00:29:28,441 --> 00:29:35,932
Here we have a mixed breed dog, probably about 40 pounds in weight that we're waiting for a customer to come pick up that we did in the laying down, actually a

410
00:29:35,933 --> 00:29:36,780
sleeping position.

411
00:29:36,781 --> 00:29:40,940
We asked for photographs so that we could pose it how they tend to lay.

412
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:47,300
Then here we have a cat, again, same thing as far as the pose is concerned, as to how this cat liked to sleep.

413
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:48,720
The fur is very soft.

414
00:29:50,080 --> 00:29:52,800
And by combing it, they at least have it so they can feel it.

415
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:56,940
And here we have the first freeze-dried dog, which was done in 1969.

416
00:29:56,941 --> 00:30:02,280
and as you can see from it, I mean the muscle is basically turned into almost stone

417
00:30:02,281 --> 00:30:07,120
and it's a very good way to really show how durable freeze-dry taxidermy is.

418
00:30:08,300 --> 00:30:12,200
While freeze-drying uses nearly every part of the specimen being mounted,

419
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:16,460
a new twist on an old method in taxidermy is gaining popularity.

420
00:30:17,100 --> 00:30:19,880
It utilizes no part of the hunted animal or fish

421
00:30:19,881 --> 00:30:24,160
and often takes death completely out of the taxidermy equation.

422
00:30:24,161 --> 00:30:29,700
Pet taxidermy was a popular custom in 19th century England.

423
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:36,160
No proper Victorian home was complete without a glass case filled with stuffed cats, dogs, and birds.

424
00:30:36,980 --> 00:30:39,380
Taxidermy will return on Modern Marvels.

425
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:42,800
Return to taxidermy on Modern Marvels.

426
00:30:45,560 --> 00:30:50,740
At the base of the Orama Falls in Venezuela, an angler makes a rare catch.

427
00:30:50,741 --> 00:30:51,441
fish.

428
00:30:51,442 --> 00:31:01,560
This prehistoric looking payara fish will be weighed, photographed, and then let

429
00:31:01,561 --> 00:31:02,261
go.

430
00:31:02,262 --> 00:31:14,140
It's the job of experts like Mike Kirkhart in Stewart, Florida, to reproduce these fish

431
00:31:14,141 --> 00:31:18,180
using no parts of the actual fish.

432
00:31:18,181 --> 00:31:23,280
The body and teeth are fiberglass, the eyes are glass.

433
00:31:23,281 --> 00:31:26,800
When color is applied to the fiberglass payara with an airbrush,

434
00:31:26,801 --> 00:31:30,600
the result is an uncanny representation.

435
00:31:30,601 --> 00:31:34,840
An offshoot of traditional taxidermy is mold reproduction.

436
00:31:34,841 --> 00:31:39,660
No skins or actual parts of the alligator were used in this mount.

437
00:31:39,661 --> 00:31:42,920
Likewise, this Komodo dragon is entirely fabricated,

438
00:31:42,921 --> 00:31:45,140
based on a mold created from the actual animal.

439
00:31:47,500 --> 00:31:49,540
Furless animals don't skin and mount well.

440
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:51,140
Fish are even harder.

441
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,420
Reproducing, say, sharks, which were difficult to skin mount,

442
00:31:55,560 --> 00:31:59,720
is where fiberglass probably was most used in the early days.

443
00:32:00,300 --> 00:32:01,420
They have no bone structure.

444
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:04,240
There's a lot of cartilage in fin areas.

445
00:32:04,241 --> 00:32:09,420
There's too much collapse, oil shrinkage, difficulty in managing the skin.

446
00:32:09,421 --> 00:32:19,520
This rhino head is also cast in fiberglass, a popular alternative trophy for the hunter who can no longer afford the permits to hunt and kill big game.

447
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:27,040
What we do now is we do a reproduction for the fellas that go out and dart the animals.

448
00:32:27,640 --> 00:32:32,960
They actually spend anywhere between $6,000 to $9,000 to dart a live animal.

449
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:38,340
They take their picture, and then normally, as all they would have for their dollar, was that photograph.

450
00:32:38,341 --> 00:32:45,640
Well, now we can take and duplicate the horn into the size that they have off of the pitcher.

451
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:50,940
And so then we can transfer that size onto this particular piece,

452
00:32:51,380 --> 00:32:55,160
completely paint it up with our airbrushing fellows over in the studio,

453
00:32:55,440 --> 00:32:58,500
and give them something for their wall besides a pitcher.

454
00:33:00,240 --> 00:33:05,320
Nowhere in taxidermy has the fiberglass mount been more accepted than in the world of sport fishing.

455
00:33:05,321 --> 00:33:10,420
40 years ago they went out caught fish and the day was all about bringing every

456
00:33:10,421 --> 00:33:17,220
fish you could home. It became pretty clear that we need to reconsider keeping

457
00:33:17,221 --> 00:33:20,800
everything that we caught and there's been a lot of limitations some of their

458
00:33:20,801 --> 00:33:27,000
force some of their volunteered you know by conservation minded sportsmen.

459
00:33:27,001 --> 00:33:31,920
Creating a lifelike fish from a photo requires that a similar fish has already

460
00:33:31,921 --> 00:33:34,105
been molded and that mold is in stock.

461
00:33:34,106 --> 00:33:37,440
Fortunately for fishermen, Mike has been a prodigious mold

462
00:33:37,441 --> 00:33:38,141
maker.

463
00:33:38,142 --> 00:33:42,460
As they were limiting what they'd catch, what size you could keep, I realized I need to

464
00:33:42,461 --> 00:33:47,335
start building molds on fish and so I've built about 1,800 molds.

465
00:33:47,336 --> 00:33:49,360
These are all the different

466
00:33:49,361 --> 00:33:53,434
molds that I've built in the 27 years I've been doing this.

467
00:33:53,435 --> 00:33:55,920
So we've got everything from turtles

468
00:33:55,921 --> 00:33:59,720
A largemouth bass that would weigh about 550 pounds,

469
00:34:00,240 --> 00:34:03,440
over to a giant sawfish that would be 700, 800 pounds.

470
00:34:03,580 --> 00:34:04,820
It's 18 feet long.

471
00:34:05,460 --> 00:34:08,680
A giant marlin that are in the thousands of pound range,

472
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:11,980
1,000 to 1,700 pounds is our largest.

473
00:34:13,420 --> 00:34:15,800
Mike continues to add to his mold collection.

474
00:34:16,020 --> 00:34:20,280
This is a garfish, which Mike poses in sand before covering with plaster.

475
00:34:20,281 --> 00:34:27,280
The whole creative artistic process of fiberglass reproduction is in the mold building.

476
00:34:27,281 --> 00:34:32,280
So when you get a specimen, I use sand to actually support the shaping that I want of the fish

477
00:34:32,281 --> 00:34:36,280
to make it look like it's swimming or at rest, but natural.

478
00:34:36,281 --> 00:34:43,280
And in using the sand, I create the separation point of the two halves of the mold that's to be made.

479
00:34:43,281 --> 00:34:48,280
Interesting things like the position of the mouth, the position of the gills,

480
00:34:48,281 --> 00:34:53,840
positions of the fins are all in the ability to be able to hold that positioning of that

481
00:34:53,841 --> 00:34:54,980
fish in place.

482
00:34:54,981 --> 00:34:58,560
And there's a million tricks and ways that it can be done.

483
00:34:59,960 --> 00:35:04,840
When it's dry, the fish and mold will be flipped over and the second side of the mold will

484
00:35:04,841 --> 00:35:05,541
be poured.

485
00:35:06,960 --> 00:35:11,580
Plaster casts are eventually reproduced in fiberglass, which are durable and allow for

486
00:35:11,581 --> 00:35:13,420
endless reproductions of a single fish.

487
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:16,460
This is a great white shark fins.

488
00:35:16,461 --> 00:35:21,460
What she's doing right now is actually putting liquid resin in.

489
00:35:21,461 --> 00:35:25,460
It's a liquid that goes to a solid.

490
00:35:25,461 --> 00:35:31,460
What she's doing now is throwing the reinforcement material, the fiberglass, in with the resin.

491
00:35:31,461 --> 00:35:35,460
Once it's cured, then the two halves of this mold will go together.

492
00:35:35,461 --> 00:35:37,460
It'll cure.

493
00:35:37,461 --> 00:35:44,460
And then we'll pop that apart, and out will come a casting of the fin of this great white shark.

494
00:35:44,461 --> 00:35:48,760
This is the work area where we do all the dirty work.

495
00:35:49,500 --> 00:35:51,540
You know, the finishing, assembly, the grinding,

496
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:55,720
transforming these castings from a raw casting

497
00:35:55,721 --> 00:35:58,540
into the finished product before it gets painted.

498
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:03,720
Right now, Buddy is preparing to set some teeth into a payara,

499
00:36:04,220 --> 00:36:06,200
one of the fish that we caught down in Venezuela.

500
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:08,740
The final step is painting.

501
00:36:09,260 --> 00:36:12,840
Airbrushes, big guns, lots of paint, lots of air moving.

502
00:36:12,841 --> 00:36:14,800
So it's a kind of interesting process.

503
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:18,940
A lot of people compliment me with my ability,

504
00:36:19,060 --> 00:36:21,220
and I tell them that anybody can do this.

505
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:23,460
I suppose it's like playing a musical instrument,

506
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:25,340
putting up time into it, and your heart's there.

507
00:36:25,660 --> 00:36:26,880
Airbrushing is no different.

508
00:36:28,840 --> 00:36:32,640
The finished product provides the fisherman his trophy, his memory,

509
00:36:33,160 --> 00:36:35,420
while preserving the species for future fishing.

510
00:36:37,660 --> 00:36:39,700
Sportsmen are, in increasing numbers,

511
00:36:39,701 --> 00:36:42,500
finding this an acceptable trade-off in taxidermy.

512
00:36:42,840 --> 00:36:45,620
And they tend to be the ones that want to release the fish

513
00:36:45,621 --> 00:36:49,340
and are very comfortable with the idea of, you know, taking a photograph,

514
00:36:49,780 --> 00:36:53,520
letting the fish go, and then contacting someone like myself

515
00:36:53,521 --> 00:36:58,800
as opposed to killing the fish and dragging it into whoever locally would accept the job.

516
00:36:59,760 --> 00:37:04,420
Fiberglass reproduction is one practice changing the look and methodology of taxidermy.

517
00:37:04,860 --> 00:37:10,160
Another is plastination, a breakthrough in body preservation that has people lining up in museums.

518
00:37:10,161 --> 00:37:13,940
The specimens on exhibit are breaking new ground as well.

519
00:37:14,900 --> 00:37:16,160
They're human beings.

520
00:37:18,320 --> 00:37:23,500
A fiberglass reproduction of a 1,700-pound marlin weighs only 75 pounds.

521
00:37:24,120 --> 00:37:26,320
Taxidermy will return on Modern Marvels.

522
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:30,560
Now return to Taxidermy on Modern Marvels.

523
00:37:30,561 --> 00:37:44,500
the exhibit is called body worlds and it has been breaking attendance records in

524
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:50,320
Asia Europe and now the United States the posed bodies are human a fascination

525
00:37:50,321 --> 00:37:54,520
that some taxidermists have had for centuries the 19th century French

526
00:37:54,521 --> 00:37:59,560
taxidermist Louis de Fran wrote about how to do it which suggests that at one

527
00:37:59,561 --> 00:38:02,660
time or another, he experimented with taxidermy on humans.

528
00:38:02,661 --> 00:38:09,800
He describes how the human body is not at all conducive to taxidermy technology.

529
00:38:09,801 --> 00:38:15,840
The opaqueness of our skin, the lack of ability to recreate the anatomy, we're always confronted

530
00:38:15,841 --> 00:38:17,300
with human form.

531
00:38:17,301 --> 00:38:22,880
So to see the human form in that grotesque condition, in that grotesque of a shape,

532
00:38:22,881 --> 00:38:28,080
it could make anybody steer away from even wanting to go near a taxidermy and using that

533
00:38:28,081 --> 00:38:30,040
process in preserving the human body.

534
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:33,860
East German-born anatomist Dr. Gunter von Hagens

535
00:38:33,861 --> 00:38:37,160
has not only discovered how to preserve and present human beings,

536
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:40,280
he's proven that the public has a great interest in their display.

537
00:38:42,340 --> 00:38:43,760
I'm a kind of showman.

538
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:46,480
Every good teacher is a kind of showman.

539
00:38:47,020 --> 00:38:50,520
I have to present something in a very interesting,

540
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:52,900
very lifelike way.

541
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:56,760
What I actually do is I try

542
00:38:56,761 --> 00:39:01,560
to open the hearts of the people to themselves, to their own body.

543
00:39:03,120 --> 00:39:06,360
Like their animal counterparts in the great museums of this country,

544
00:39:06,960 --> 00:39:09,300
these displayed humans are on a mission to educate.

545
00:39:10,360 --> 00:39:14,020
You have to recognize yourself as a specimen, not this is a specimen.

546
00:39:14,021 --> 00:39:16,960
Oh, this could be myself.

547
00:39:17,360 --> 00:39:20,820
Actually, this is responsible for the big success of body worlds.

548
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:25,240
Seeing firsthand the effects of smoking on the lung

549
00:39:25,241 --> 00:39:28,180
has made a real impact on some that visit the exhibit.

550
00:39:28,940 --> 00:39:33,340
As independent surveys show, people smoke to 10% less or not anymore,

551
00:39:33,420 --> 00:39:36,100
50% think more of their health,

552
00:39:36,520 --> 00:39:41,900
and 30% are more likely to donate their organs for organ donation programs.

553
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:47,380
Dr. von Hagen uses a new process called plastination

554
00:39:47,381 --> 00:39:51,180
to preserve not only skin and hair, but also muscles and organs,

555
00:39:51,480 --> 00:39:53,320
even the circulatory and nervous systems.

556
00:39:53,321 --> 00:39:59,782
70% of our body consists of water. Water gives life, water decay is life.

557
00:39:59,783 --> 00:40:02,260
No water, no life, no decay.

558
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:11,860
In plastination, water is removed and specimens are impregnated with a reactive polymer like silicon rubber, epoxy, or polyester resin.

559
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,669
Plastinate specimens are dry, odorless, and durable.

560
00:40:15,670 --> 00:40:19,260
They retain their cellular identity down to the microscopic level.

561
00:40:19,261 --> 00:40:25,260
Like here's the tongue, here would be my lung, the smoker's lung, that's not my lung,

562
00:40:25,261 --> 00:40:30,260
and here's the heart, the liver, the stomach, all here, the intestine.

563
00:40:30,261 --> 00:40:33,260
So it shows where the specimens are situated.

564
00:40:35,260 --> 00:40:40,260
Like the traditional taxidermist, von Hagen's attempts to recreate life through the posing of the specimen.

565
00:40:41,260 --> 00:40:43,260
A dancer stands on point.

566
00:40:44,260 --> 00:40:46,260
A soccer player is frozen mid-kick.

567
00:40:46,261 --> 00:40:49,900
and a skateboarder handstands inside a half pipe.

568
00:40:51,580 --> 00:40:56,100
The macabre sense of humor sometimes present in taxidermy is at work here, too.

569
00:40:56,860 --> 00:40:59,920
This harrowing-looking figure has been dubbed Wingman.

570
00:41:00,680 --> 00:41:05,320
His anatomy has been flayed outward to give visitors yet another way of viewing the human body.

571
00:41:06,300 --> 00:41:11,580
The creator added a man's straw hat to the figure in an effort to make him less frightening to children.

572
00:41:11,581 --> 00:41:16,580
Both the rider and the horse were once very much alive.

573
00:41:16,581 --> 00:41:24,580
The Cheying Horse's rider is the example for comparative anatomy at its best.

574
00:41:24,581 --> 00:41:30,580
To show the human brain compared to the small horse brain.

575
00:41:30,581 --> 00:41:37,580
And the opposite, to show the big muscle of the horse and the female muscles of the human body.

576
00:41:37,581 --> 00:41:38,292
human body.

577
00:41:38,293 --> 00:41:43,920
The exhibit is designed to showcase the different functional systems of the human body.

578
00:41:45,080 --> 00:41:47,060
It begins with the muscles and movement,

579
00:41:49,560 --> 00:41:53,000
then presents the brain and its elaborate network of nerves.

580
00:41:55,340 --> 00:42:00,720
The circulatory system can be viewed intact, but completely separate from its former human host.

581
00:42:00,721 --> 00:42:04,160
To represent the reproduction system, Dr.

582
00:42:04,161 --> 00:42:08,020
Von Hagen displays two truly shocking figures.

583
00:42:08,800 --> 00:42:12,160
The woman and her fetus both died during the eighth month of pregnancy.

584
00:42:13,100 --> 00:42:18,560
There could be no better specimens to illustrate that than a pregnant woman.

585
00:42:19,120 --> 00:42:25,880
And when I got the specimen, by full consent, I knew this has to go into the exhibition.

586
00:42:26,580 --> 00:42:28,980
The pregnant woman and child may move onlookers,

587
00:42:29,240 --> 00:42:32,298
but perhaps the most moving plastinate in this exhibit for Dr.

588
00:42:32,299 --> 00:42:33,580
von Hagen is less noticed.

589
00:42:34,300 --> 00:42:36,980
This is a man he describes as his best friend.

590
00:42:37,680 --> 00:42:45,640
To plastinate my best friend, it was at first to fulfill his last wish, his testimony.

591
00:42:46,720 --> 00:42:54,260
He wanted to become a plastinate because he felt to be too young to end on a cemetery.

592
00:42:54,261 --> 00:42:59,400
He had a malignant kidney tumor, so he knew he had to live maximum of one year.

593
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:06,660
When dissecting him, I needed about three times as much time,

594
00:43:07,120 --> 00:43:08,660
but it was good for my morning work.

595
00:43:09,080 --> 00:43:15,280
It was like he stretched his hand across the barrier of death to me,

596
00:43:15,640 --> 00:43:19,760
and so I got in peace with him.

597
00:43:19,761 --> 00:43:26,900
With plastination, taxidermy may finally achieve an age-old goal.

598
00:43:26,901 --> 00:43:31,720
While the very best in traditional taxidermy begins to wear out in a few hundred years,

599
00:43:32,360 --> 00:43:36,180
the plastination process may produce displays that are virtually immortal.

600
00:43:37,400 --> 00:43:41,400
Plastinates will last a didactic eternity,

601
00:43:42,060 --> 00:43:48,280
which means they will last forever as long as there's a temperature below 50 centigrade.

602
00:43:49,760 --> 00:43:50,829
And one day, Dr.

603
00:43:50,830 --> 00:43:57,980
Von Hagen plans to make the ultimate contribution to the science of body preservation and display, himself.

604
00:43:57,981 --> 00:44:06,580
I will die one day and my body should be on earth for some time to come.

605
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:17,620
I'm in peace with my plastinates because I know that every specimen will become something unique as every body donor in this life or something very special.

606
00:44:17,621 --> 00:44:22,440
The methods may have evolved, but the reactions have not.

607
00:44:23,060 --> 00:44:25,500
Some are intrigued, others are repulsed.

608
00:44:26,080 --> 00:44:32,300
But taxidermy continues to confront the viewer with the enduring mysteries of life and death.

